This is a very common and highly realistic roadblock when working with medium-sized businesses and contractors in Ghana. The Ghana Revenue Authority (GRA) has significantly intensified its digital tracking, checking corporate bank accounts, and monitoring high-visibility social media marketing to audit businesses for VAT and corporate tax compliance.
If your client is terrified of appearing on the GRA's radar via public sponsored ads, you must completely pivot away from "loud" inbound digital marketing. Instead, shift to a "Silent/Ghost" Inbound Strategy or High-Value B2B Account-Based Marketing (ABM).
1. The "Ghost" Lead Generation Strategy
You can generate high-ticket glazing leads without ever publishing an ad containing the client's business name, phone number, or recognizable branding.
Anonymous "Industry" Lead Generation Funnels: Instead of running ads from the client's official Facebook page, create a generic, unbranded industry portal page (e.g., "Ghana Modern Architecture Network" or "Accra Premium Building Solutions").
Run ads from this generic entity offering a free catalog or service: "Download the 2027 Guide to Frameless Glass Costs in Accra" or "Get quotes from top verified glass fabricators."
When a developer or homeowner fills out the form, you receive the lead privately. You then hand-deliver the lead to your client as a pre-vetted referral. The GRA only sees a generic marketing page, not your client’s business.
Private WhatsApp Group Scraping & Networking: In Ghana, a massive amount of construction business happens in private WhatsApp groups (e.g., estate developer groups, contractor networks, hardware supplier associations).
Offer to manually embed yourself or a virtual assistant into these networks to monitor requests for glazing sub-contracts and pitch your client privately via DM.
2. High-Value B2B Account-Based Marketing (The Best Pivot)
Glazing is a high-ticket service. A glazing company doesn’t need 500 random retail leads; they need 5 solid relationships with estate developers, main contractors, and architects who manage multiple projects a year. This can be done entirely via private, direct outreach.
Physical "Site Mapping" Safari:
Instead of running ads, you (or a hired hand) physically scout or map out rapidly growing residential and commercial construction zones in Accra/Kumasi (e.g., East Legon Hills, Oyarifa, Airport Residential, Tse Addo, Pokuase).
Identify buildings that have reached the structural stage where windows and facades are about to be fitted.
Walk onto the site, ask for the Project Manager, Site Engineer, or Foreperson, and hand them a physical, premium printed portfolio. Collect their direct WhatsApp number.
Architect & Quantity Surveyor (QS) Direct Pitching: Architects and QS professionals draw up the specifications for buildings. If your client gets specified in the bill of quantities, they win the job automatically.
Build a curated list of 50 local architectural firms and independent surveyors in Ghana.
Send personalized, private messages via LinkedIn or WhatsApp, or arrange brief physical drop-ins to present a professional catalog of your client’s commercial glazing capabilities.
